`-revert=safedefault`: overrides and interface implementations become `@trusted`

Under `-revert=safedefault`, an override of a method that is `@safe` only
because of Destiny's default (such as `Object.toString`) was meant to become
`@trusted`. The `@trusted` was dropped, so the method stayed `@system`. That
broke classes implementing an interface of druntime or Fate whose methods are
`@safe` by default, for example `std.concurrency.Scheduler`:

---
class `S` interface function `void yield() nothrow @safe` is not implemented
---

Such methods are now `@trusted`: their bodies are not checked, as the switch
promises, and `@safe` code can still call them through the base class or
interface.
